In article <1990Jul2.180330.24199@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG> xanthian@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG (Kent Paul Dolan) writes:
>Sure, let's make the game a little easier to manage and go to the rogue 7
>plan, eight ring fingers.  Swapping rings on and off is a bloody nuisance,
>especially when I really need the qualities provided by three or four and
>haven't found a way to gain the equivalent intrinsic qualities yet.

You have completely misunderstood what I was saying. I am not trying to make
the game easier, I am trying to make the game less stressing and less
frustrating. Have to type the same command every 30 seconds is a pain, and
you're bound to make a keying error eventually and do something wrong, just
because you have to use a mechanical device to tell the computer what to do,
and there's no way to correct mistakes. If you were to say "ok put on the
ring now" you would never make a mistake, because by mispronouncing you
could never say "ok put on the cursed blindfold now". Your remark about
eight ring fingers proves that you have not read what I said carefully enough.
I made it clear that the saving in keystrokes from the point of view of the
human player would be obtained at the cost of one ring finger, which might
have been used for something else if the human player were patient enough to
shuffle rings in and out. How you conclude from this that I am proposing more
ring fingers is something I fail to understand.

>Would this be an instant game balance destroyer?  It takes one of the
>existing game balance mechanisms and strategic parts of the game out;

Strategic??? Very complicated strategy: "when hp < maximum hp, put the ring
on, otherwise put it off, unless you're engaged in some fighting or suchlike
which you obviously don't want to interrupt". There is NEVER any question as
to what I want to do; there's just the weary hand holding the shift key a bit
too long. Similarly, one of my characters became a cannibal because it had 5
rations of food as 'y', and I typed 'ey' as usual before realizing there was
a soldier corpse there. THIS is the kind of mistakes which we should strive
to eliminate - when the intent of the human being is turned into something
else by a finger slip.

A couple days ago, after pressing the '>' key, I entered a ghost level. After
disposing of the housecat, a '&' appeared. Bummer. 'The bone devil summonsr
help!'. Re-bummer, 2 more '&' appear. 'The bone devil picks up a sword named
Stormbringer'. I'll let you imagine the rest. This bone level keeps coming
back from time to time, because I keep getting killed when I reach it. I've
been trying to find a way to escape to the next level (I guess the simplest
solution is to arrange to grab the sword before them, and then run away
before they get me). This makes the game difficult, but THIS kind of
difficulty is what makes nethack interesting. Trying to train myself to become
a better typist and avoid typos is not what I would call interesting (most of
the time I use an editor which is happy to let me correct my mistakes), and
losing a "good" game because of such an error is frustrating and not
educational in any way.

  Eric


